From: MiaAI_Lab <mia.ailab1@gmail.com>
Subject: glm: warm turns, so the next user message resumes past the agent loop

GLM's template renders an assistant turn's reasoning only after the last user message (clear_thinking). During an
agent loop the loop's turns carry their reasoning; once the next user message comes they render with an empty think
block, so the prompt changes at the first turn after the previous user message and the prefix cache resumes there:
the whole previous turn (tool calls and results, 50-100K tokens on this pair) is prefilled again, 10-30 s before the
first token. Measured 2026-11-04 on the serving pair: a user message after a small tool loop resumed at 4,096 of
9,335 tokens; with the loop's turns rendered the same way it resumed at 8,192.

The server already has the mechanism: warm.zig prefills, after a reply, the conversation plus the reply plus the next
turn's opening as a background prompt-only pass (max_tokens 0), on engines that report warm_turns. That rendering
places the reply before a next user message, so it is the cleared one the next message sends. The GLM family did not
report warm_turns: native/cuda.zig enables them for a family that declares warm_quiet, and GLM declared none.

Each pass's outcome is logged ("next turn: background prefill of N tokens done", "stepped aside: other work came
first" when it never started, "stepped aside" when it yielded mid-pass, "failed: ..."): a pass that keeps nothing
was invisible before.

- families/glm/cuda_native.zig declares warm_quiet (150 ms, Nemotron's): the pass waits that long for other work
  before its first chunk.
- families/glm/cuda_lanes.zig: the chunk gate's cancel check also reads the stream's yield check, so a warm pass steps
  aside at its next chunk gate once a request waits or a stream decodes (lane_host.yieldsTo). The engine already keeps
  the states the pass reached on a cancelled chunk (snapshots.interrupted through the marks), on every rank, by rank
  0's word (engine.gate's shared cancel). A foreground request's cancel check is unchanged: it never yields.
- native/cuda.zig: the host takes the family's warm_quiet. Before, a family with warm turns got Nemotron's constant.
- server/warm.zig: after a reply with tool calls the next message is a tool result, not a user message, so the dummy
  next turn is a tool message; a template that clears earlier turns' reasoning renders the loop differently before
  each, and a user-turn rendering after a call would prefill the cleared loop on every tool call. After an answer the
  dummy turn stays a user message.
- server/openai.zig: the warm message carries the reply's reasoning_content, as the client sends it back, so a tool
  turn's rendering (reasoning kept) and a clear_thinking=false client's match the next request.
- core/lane_host.zig (every family): a warm pass's quiet wait and its yield check saw a foreground request only once
  queued or decoding. One admitted ahead of the pass into the fill queue (a chunk a round, how this family reads
  prompts beside decoders) was neither, so a whole-prompt warm pass could hold up that prefill for its length. Both
  checks now also read the admitted list; lane_host_warm_test.zig has the case (it failed before the change: the
  pass ran to its end ahead of the request).

Tests: late_system_test.zig (a tool-call reply warms the tool turn's opening, a plain reply the user turn's; the
existing warm-prompt tests pass unchanged), lane_host_warm_test.zig (the admitted-ahead case; 404 lane host tests
pass), check-native-cuda-host compiles the family. The pass's behaviour on the pair (a user message after a loop resuming
past the loop, the pass yielding to a request) is verified live after the restart: rank0.log's "resumed at" for the
next user message, and the reply's cached_tokens.

Found and fixed by Mia's AI Lab with a council of three: the lab's agent, GLM-5.3-Flash on this pair (it checked the TP2, snapshot
and page paths) and Codex (it found the fill-queue hole). Their records: docs/council-2026-11-04/ in the recipe.

